#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Split the Tax App's hub files into one module per form.

Usage:
    tools/split-hubs [--check] [ROOT]     split ROOT (default: the current checkout)
    tools/split-hubs --adopt [SPLIT]      bring a branch made before the split onto it

--check changes nothing and exits 1 when a run would change the tree.

What it does. Each hub in HUBS keeps the items shared by many forms; every other
top-level item (struct, enum, fn, const, type, trait, a whole impl block, or one
method of a hub type's inherent impl such as `impl Interview`) whose name matches a
form's names moves, with its leading comments and attributes, to that form's module
`src/<owner area>/<form>_<suffix>.rs`. The hub declares it at its end (`#[path] mod`
plus a glob re-export), so every existing path (`crate::federal_model::W2`,
`Interview::hsa_page`) still resolves; the module starts with `use super::*;`, so
the moved text compiles unchanged in the hub's scope. Text moves byte for byte
except the rewrite that keeps visibility identical one module deeper: none ->
`pub(super)`, `pub(super)` -> the hub parent's, on the item, a moved struct's fields
and a moved inherent impl's members. An impl follows its self type; a split impl
keeps its header in each module. `use`, `mod`, macros, macro invocations and items
using `self::`/`super::` paths stay in the hub.

Deterministic and idempotent: the output depends only on the hub text and the
tables below. A second run changes nothing; a new hub item whose name matches a
form (e.g. a new `W2...` type or `hsa_...` page method) is appended to its form's
module, so new work lands in per-form files. rustfmt runs on the files written.

Bringing an open branch up to main (do not just run the tool and merge: the form
modules would be added on both sides with no common base, so every difference
would conflict):
    tools/split-hubs --adopt          # for each split on origin/main the branch lacks,
                                      # oldest first: merge main just before it (stop on a
                                      # conflict: resolve, commit, rerun), then merge the
                                      # split with this branch split by that split's tool
    git merge origin/main             # base is the last split: only real overlaps conflict
Run from main's copy if your branch lacks the tool:
    git show origin/main:tools/split-hubs > /tmp/split-hubs && python3 /tmp/split-hubs --adopt
Splits so far: the model/page/test hubs, then federal_rules.rs. Every commit that changes
this tool must be a split: the tool's output on its parent plus the tool, nothing else.
A branch started after the last split merges main normally; run the tool (and commit)
first only when it added items to a hub.

To give a form its module, add its names to the tables and rerun; to keep a new
item shared, give it a name no form matches. Names are exact or globs (`*`).
"""

